            {
                "entry_id": "fdf5593d-cd3a-302f-a10e-bd894bc8397a",
                "word": "Cimmerian",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "0f2d2af6-7295-4d7e-b77d-b42f53c2e9ec",
                        "entry_id": "fdf5593d-cd3a-302f-a10e-bd894bc8397a",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "Cimmerians"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "ea5094ae-e936-3382-b199-ce6599c3f20f",
                        "entry_id": "fdf5593d-cd3a-302f-a10e-bd894bc8397a",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Any of the mythical people supposed to inhabit a land of perpetual darkness."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "Greek"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "275b9bdd-d6fe-4619-a9cb-ae1fa6665b64",
                                "sense_id": "ea5094ae-e936-3382-b199-ce6599c3f20f",
                                "text": "The city, there, of the Cimmerians stands\nWith clouds and darkness veil’d, on whom the sun\nDeigns not to look with his beam-darting eye,"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "ff23d6f4-a105-4350-acc8-7dc55f698d7c",
                                "sense_id": "ea5094ae-e936-3382-b199-ce6599c3f20f",
                                "text": "On their mysterious shores were the improbable homes of impossible peoples. The Great Sea, the Broad Sea, the Boundless Sea; the Ethiopians, \"dwelling far away, the most distant of men,\" and the Cimmerians, \"covered with darkness and cloud,\" where \"baleful night is spread over timid mortals.\""
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "9f6812e2-99d9-32a3-aafd-587128e6d5ed",
                        "entry_id": "fdf5593d-cd3a-302f-a10e-bd894bc8397a",
                        "glosses": [
                            "One of the Cimmerii, ancient equestrian nomads of Indo-European origin."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "d0153227-ad81-4114-abb3-2cbf171dc4eb",
                                "sense_id": "9f6812e2-99d9-32a3-aafd-587128e6d5ed",
                                "text": "1910, Herodotus (484 B.C.E.–425 B.C.E.), History of Herodotus, translated by George Rawlinson\nIn his reign the Cimmerians, driven from their homes by the nomads of Scythia, entered Asia and captured Sardis"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "58cae4d4-33fe-41b0-88bb-2b04987d90fe",
                                "sense_id": "9f6812e2-99d9-32a3-aafd-587128e6d5ed",
                                "text": "Believe me, queen, your swarth Cimmerian\nDoth make your honour of his body's hue,\nSpotted, detested, and abominable."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "d8c1bf33-98f6-440d-8959-563267f130e9",
                                "sense_id": "9f6812e2-99d9-32a3-aafd-587128e6d5ed",
                                "text": "The Phrygian power was broken in the 9th or 8th century B.C. by the Cimmerians, who entered Asia Minor through Armenia"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "75e7a966-c886-33e0-87e0-b94dedc5bdad",
                        "entry_id": "fdf5593d-cd3a-302f-a10e-bd894bc8397a",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The prophetic priestess presiding over the Apollonian Oracle at Cimmerium in Italy."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "historical"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "aa67c950-e660-4984-b927-d4dd30df2da7",
                                "sense_id": "75e7a966-c886-33e0-87e0-b94dedc5bdad",
                                "text": "1867 to 1885, Lactantius (240–320 C.E.), Ante-Nicene Fathers, translated by William Fletcher\nVarro relates that there were ten Sibyls,—the first of the Persians, the second the Libyan, the third the Delphian, the fourth the Cimmerian..."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "d263efc8-58a4-4201-bb40-f4741f6bf61b",
                        "source_entry_id": "fdf5593d-cd3a-302f-a10e-bd894bc8397a",
                        "source_sense_description": "any of the mythical people supposed to inhabit a land of perpetual darkness",
